<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-intro" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Starting with Data</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      We start this chapter - and book therefore - with an exercise.
      It's a different start to what may usually be expected, but there's good
      reasons for it.
      We haven't introduced any techniques, and so we are going purely off what
      we already know about data (which may be little, or may be surprising).
      Let's just try it out, and see what can we do with a dataset by looking at
      it carefully and without instruction.
      The remainder of the chapter then introduces the structure of the book,
      and relates the exercise to the chapters that follow.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-first-exploration">
    <title>A First Exploration</title>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-the-data">
      <title>The dataset</title>

      <p>
        Most of the data in this book are real data, but we start with some
        simulated data.
        But, let's start with some background to this simulated data.
        Between 2021 and 2023, measurements were recorded from flamingos at
        three lakes in Kenya's Rift Valley: Lake Bogoria, Lake Elementaita, and
        Lake Nakuru.
        The dataset <c>kenya_flamingos</c> contains one row per bird, with the
        columns described in <xref ref="table-flamingo-columns"/>.
        You can access the data in R through the <c>statsdata</c> package
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        library(pak) # you may need to install this if you have not yet, with install.packages("pak")

        pak::pak("PreTeXtBooks/statsdata")
        library(statsdata)

        data(kenya_flamingos)
        </code>
      </program>

      <table xml:id="table-flamingo-columns">
        <title>Columns in <c>kenya_flamingos.csv</c></title>

        <tabular halign="left">
          <col halign="left" width="25%"/>
          <col halign="left" width="75%"/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell>Column</cell>
            <cell>Description</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell><c>lake</c></cell>
            <cell>Lake at which the bird was recorded</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell><c>species</c></cell>
            <cell>Greater Flamingo or Lesser Flamingo</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell><c>bill_length_mm</c></cell>
            <cell>Bill length, in millimetres</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell><c>bill_depth_mm</c></cell>
            <cell>Bill depth, in millimetres</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell><c>leg_length_mm</c></cell>
            <cell>Leg length, in millimetres</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell><c>body_mass_g</c></cell>
            <cell>Body mass, in grams</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell><c>sex</c></cell>
            <cell>Recorded sex of the bird</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell><c>year</c></cell>
            <cell>Year of measurement</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        The dataset is small enough to be examined in a spreadsheet, in R, or on
        paper.
        The choice of tool does not affect the exercise, it is entirely up to
        you.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-first-exploration">
      <title>Thirty minutes with the data</title>

      <p>
        Examine the flamingo dataset for thirty minutes and record three
        observations that you got from the data.
        These can be any three things that you can gleam from the data - it can
        be as simple or complex as you like: the number of rows, the number of
        missing values, a graph or summary, frequency of birds in each lake or
        species.
        The three things are not important.
        They don't even need to be correct!
        They just need to be three things you can see from the data.
      </p>

      <p>
        Record the three observations before reading the discussion that
        follows.
        The discussion lists observations made by other readers, and reading it
        first removes the purpose of the exercise.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-results-ish">
    <title>What the Data Showed</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        This section is the other half of the exercise.
        Having spent thirty minutes with the flamingo data and written down
        three observations, you can now compare what you found with what other
        readers found.
        The point is not to check your answers against a correct list; there is
        no correct list.
        The point is to see how much a dataset gives up to someone who simply
        looks at it, and to notice that almost every observation raises a
        further question that looking alone cannot settle.
        Those further questions are what the rest of the book is about.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="what-did-others-find">
      <title>What did others find?</title>

      <introduction>
        <p>
          There are many different things you might have noticed while exploring
          the data.
          Let us know on the Discussion GitHub page what you found.
          Here are some examples.
          If you did not find any of these things then that does not matter.
          This book looks at how to do all of these explorations!
        </p>
      </introduction>

      <subsubsection>
        <title>How much data?</title>

        <p>
          There are eight columns and 344 rows in the data.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
data("kenya_flamingos")

View(kenya_flamingos)
          </code>
        </program>
      </subsubsection>

      <subsubsection>
        <title>What about bill depth?</title>

        <p>
          Bill depth varies from 18.10 mm to 33.90 mm.
          There are four missing values.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
summary(kenya_flamingos$bill_depth_mm)
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          We can see several features of the data from this simple summary: the
          smallest and largest values, some values in between, the mean, and the
          number of missing values.
        </p>
      </subsubsection>

      <subsubsection>
        <title>How many of each species?</title>

        <p>
          There are two species in the data: Greater Flamingos and Lesser
          Flamingos.
          Lesser Flamingos outnumber Greater Flamingos by a substantial margin.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
table(kenya_flamingos$species)
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          This gives us:
        </p>
        <pre>
Greater Flamingo  Lesser Flamingo
             66              278
        </pre>
        <p>
          But the counts reflect which birds were measured, not which birds were
          present.
          Perhaps there happened to be more Lesser Flamingos present on the day
          the data were collected.
        </p>
      </subsubsection>

      <subsubsection>
        <title>Comparing body mass between species</title>

        <p>
          We can also compare the body mass of the two species.
          One simple way to do this is to calculate the average body mass for
          each species.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
library(dplyr)

kenya_flamingos %&gt;%
  group_by(species) %&gt;%
  summarise(mean(body_mass_g, na.rm = TRUE))
          </code>
        </program>
        <pre>
# A tibble: 2 x 2
  species           mean(body_mass_g, na.rm = TRUE)
  &lt;chr&gt;                              &lt;dbl&gt;
1 Greater Flamingo                    3120
2 Lesser Flamingo                     1689
        </pre>
        <p>
          Greater Flamingos are heavier than Lesser Flamingos by roughly a
          factor of two.
        </p>

        <p>
          The difference is large enough that any statement about
          <q>flamingos</q> in general will depend heavily on the mix of species
          in the data.
        </p>
      </subsubsection>

      <subsubsection>
        <title>Sorting by sex</title>

        <p>
          There are 170 females and 167 males in the data, but seven rows have
          no recorded sex.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
table(kenya_flamingos$sex)
          </code>
        </program>
        <pre>
female   male
    170    167
        </pre>
        <p>
          Flamingos are difficult to sex from external appearance, so this is a
          plausible limitation of the data collection rather than an error.
          Any comparison between male and female birds will silently exclude
          these rows if the missing values are not dealt with explicitly.
        </p>
      </subsubsection>

      <subsubsection>
        <title>Counting rows by lake</title>

        <p>
          The three lakes are not equally represented in the data.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
table(kenya_flamingos$lake)
          </code>
        </program>
        <pre>
Bogoria Elementaita      Nakuru
    124          68         152
        </pre>
        <p>
          There are 124 measurements from Bogoria, 68 from Elementaita, and 152
          from Nakuru.
          This may reflect differences in the flamingo populations, or
          differences in how much survey effort was directed at each lake, or
          something else.
        </p>
      </subsubsection>

      <subsubsection>
        <title>Plotting bill length against body mass</title>

        <p>
          We can also look at two variables together.
          For example, what happens to body mass as bill length increases?
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
plot(kenya_flamingos$bill_length_mm,
     kenya_flamingos$body_mass_g)
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          We could also use <c>ggplot2</c> to make the same plot.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
library(ggplot2)

ggplot(kenya_flamingos,
       aes(x = bill_length_mm, y = body_mass_g)) +
  geom_point()
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          We might then add information about species to the plot.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
ggplot(kenya_flamingos,
       aes(x = bill_length_mm,
           y = body_mass_g,
           colour = species)) +
  geom_point() +
  geom_smooth(method = "lm")
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          A clear positive relationship appears: birds with longer bills tend to
          be heavier.
        </p>

        <p>
          We can also see that Greater Flamingos tend to have both a higher body
          mass and a longer bill than Lesser Flamingos.
        </p>
      </subsubsection>

      <subsubsection>
        <title>And there is much more you could find</title>

        <p>
          You might have noticed things that aren't mentioned here at all.
          For example:
          <ul>
            <li>Do males and females differ in body mass?</li>
            <li>Does bill length differ between the two species?</li>
            <li>Are the flamingos at different lakes different sizes?</li>
            <li>Is there a relationship between bill depth and bill length?</li>
            <li>Are there any unusually large or small flamingos?</li>
            <li>
              Are missing values concentrated in particular variables or groups?
            </li>

            <li>Does the balance of species differ between the three lakes?</li>
            <li>
              Does the relationship between bill length and body mass look the
              same for both species?
            </li>

            <li>Can you identify individual observations that look unusual?</li>
            <li>What questions can't this dataset answer?</li>
          </ul>
        </p>

        <p>
          You don't need to have found any particular one of these things.
          The purpose of the activity was to explore the data and see what
          questions you could ask of it.
        </p>

        <p>
          These are just some of the things people noticed when they explored
          the same data.
          <em>What did you notice?</em>
        </p>
      </subsubsection>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-what-the-exercise-covered">
      <title>What the exercise covered</title>

      <p>
        The observations available in this dataset correspond closely to the
        topics of the chapters that follow.
      </p>

      <p>
        Methods to explore the data, including techniques outlined in the R code
        in <xref ref="what-did-others-find"/> are covered in
        <xref ref="ch-exploring-data"/>.
        Missing values in the <c>sex</c> column, and the question of what to do
        about them, is the subject of <xref ref="ch-cleaning-recoding"/> and
        <xref ref="ch-handling-missing-values"/>.
        Comparisons between species, lakes, or years depend on treating those
        variables as categories, which is the subject of
        <xref ref="ch-handling-factors"/>.
        The bill length and body mass relationship, which holds across the whole
        dataset but not within each species, is an instance of the problem
        examined in <xref ref="ch-multilevel-data"/>.
        The question of how spread out a measurement is, rather than simply what
        its average value is, is the subject of <xref ref="ch-variability"/>,
        and accounting for those differences is the subject of
        <xref ref="ch-accounting-variability"/>.
        And the question of whether unequal counts across lakes reflect the
        population or the survey is a question about what the data is capable of
        answering, which arises in every chapter of this book.
      </p>

      <p>
        The exercise did not require any of the techniques introduced in those
        chapters.
        What it required was attention, and a willingness to ask whether an
        apparent pattern is what it appears to be.
        The techniques that follow are tools for answering that question; they
        are not a substitute for asking it.
        (So: Go on, be honest.
        Did you read ahead?
        It's not too late to still explore the data using your own methods and
        approaches for 30 minutes).
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-about-this-book">
    <title>About This Book</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        This book is an introduction to statistical computing with R.
        Its subject is the work that has to happen before any statistical result
        can be trusted: getting data into a usable shape, understanding how it
        was collected, deciding what to do about the parts that are missing or
        miscoded, and recognising when an apparent pattern is an artefact of the
        structure of the data rather than a feature of the world.
      </p>

      <p>
        It is written for readers who are beginning to work with real data:
        students meeting statistics for the first time in a computational
        setting, and researchers or practitioners who have data to analyse and
        want to do it well.
        The examples are drawn from real situations, many of them from Africa
        and many of them cases where the data was handled badly and the
        consequences were serious.
      </p>

      <p>
        No prior experience of R is assumed, and no mathematics beyond
        arithmetic is required.
        What is assumed is a willingness to run code, look at the output, and
        ask whether it means what it appears to mean.
        If you have used R before, the early chapters will still be worth
        reading for the way they treat the data rather than the syntax.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-structure">
      <title>Structure</title>

      <p>
        The chapters are ordered so that each one deals with a problem raised by
        the one before it.
      </p>

      <p>
        <xref ref="ch-multilevel-data"/> begins with data that has structure:
        individuals belonging to groups, where the group determines both an
        individual's outcome and how common that individual is in the data.
        This is where the most persuasive wrong conclusions come from, and it is
        worth meeting early.
        <xref ref="ch-exploring-data"/> covers the first thing to do with any
        dataset: summarising it, tabulating it, and plotting it well enough to
        know what you are holding.
        <xref ref="ch-cleaning-recoding"/> deals with data that is not yet ready
        to be asked questions of: inconsistent categories, implausible values,
        and entries that were recorded under several different names.
      </p>

      <p>
        <xref ref="ch-visualising-multidimensional-data"/> takes up the problem
        of showing several variables at once, where a table contains the answer
        but cannot display it.
        <xref ref="ch-data-shapes"/> is about the arrangement of the data
        itself, and how the same information laid out differently makes some
        questions easy and others impossible.
        <xref ref="ch-handling-factors"/> covers categorical variables: how R
        stores them, how their levels are ordered, and how much depends on
        decisions about which categories exist at all.
        <xref ref="ch-handling-missing-values"/> asks why values are missing,
        and shows that the answer changes what you are entitled to do about
        them.
      </p>

      <p>
        The final two chapters turn from the value of a measurement to its
        spread.
        <xref ref="ch-variability"/> introduces variability, why it matters at
        least as much as the average, and how to measure it.
        <xref ref="ch-accounting-variability"/> then shows how to separate
        variability that is explained by the structure of the data from
        variability that is not.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-chapter-format">
      <title>Chapter format</title>

      <p>
        Most chapters follow a common structure.
        Each opens with an account of a situation in which the topic of the
        chapter mattered in practice, frequently one in which it was handled
        badly.
        The chapter then develops the relevant ideas using data, with
        accompanying R code.
        Exercises appear at the end of each chapter.
      </p>

      <p>
        The opening accounts are not incidental.
        Statistical techniques are straightforward to apply and straightforward
        to misapply, and the distinction between the two is usually a matter of
        judgement rather than method.
        The accounts are included to develop that judgement.
      </p>

      <p>
        To follow the code you will need R, available from
        <url href="https://cran.r-project.org/">cran.r-project.org</url>, and we
        recommend also installing RStudio from
        <url href="https://posit.co/download/rstudio-desktop/">posit.co</url>,
        which provides a more comfortable environment for writing and running R
        code.
        Both are free.
      </p>

      <p>
        The datasets used in this book are distributed in the
        <c>statsdata</c> package, which is installed from GitHub rather than
        from CRAN:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
install.packages("pak") # only needed once

pak::pak("PreTeXtBooks/statsdata")
library(statsdata)
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        Each dataset is then available by name, as with
        <c>data(kenya_flamingos)</c> earlier in this chapter.
        A few examples use data downloaded directly from an external source
        instead; where that is the case, the chapter gives the link and the
        steps needed to read the file into R.
      </p>

      <p>
        Some chapters also include interactive questions, which are answered in
        the browser in the online version of the book.
        These check understanding rather than recall, and usually ask you to
        work something out from a dataset rather than to select a definition.
        In the printed and PDF versions the same questions appear as ordinary
        exercises.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>
</chapter>